@node Lua Lexical Elements
@section Lexical Elements

@subsection Keywords

Lua reserves the following keywords (case-sensitive):

@table @code
@item and, or, not
Logical operations
@item break
Loop exit
@item do, end
Block delimiters
@item else, elseif, if, then
Conditional constructs
@item false, nil, true
Literal values
@item for, in, while, repeat, until
Iteration constructs
@item function
Function definition
@item goto
Unconditional jump
@item local
Local variable declaration
@item return
Function return
@item self
Implicit method parameter
@end table

@subsection Identifiers

Identifiers are alphanumeric sequences starting with a letter or underscore:

@itemize
@item Case-sensitive
@item May contain letters, digits, underscores
@item Cannot start with a digit
@item Examples: @code{player}, @code{_private}, @code{health2}
@end itemize

@subsection Literals

@subsubsection Numbers

Lua supports decimal and hexadecimal integers, and floating-point:

@example
42
3.14
1e10
0xFF
0b1010
@end example

@subsubsection Strings

Strings use single or double quotes, or long bracket notation:

@example
"Hello"
'World'
[=[Multi-line
string]=]
@end example

Escape sequences: @code{\n}, @code{\t}, @code{\\}, @code{\"}, @code{\'}

@subsection Comments

@table @asis
@item Line comments
Begin with @code{--} and extend to end of line
@item Block comments
Delimited by @code{--[[} and @code{]]}
@end table

@example
-- This is a comment
--[[ Block comment
     spanning lines ]]--
@end example

@subsection Operators

@subsubsection Arithmetic

@table @code
@item +
Addition
@item -
Subtraction
@item *
Multiplication
@item /
Division
@item %
Modulo
@item ^
Exponentiation
@item //
Floor division (Lua 5.3+)
@end table

@subsubsection Comparison

@table @code
@item ==
Equality
@item ~=
Inequality
@item <
Less than
@item >
Greater than
@item <=
Less than or equal
@item >=
Greater than or equal
@end table

@subsubsection Logical

@table @code
@item and
Logical AND (short-circuit)
@item or
Logical OR (short-circuit)
@item not
Logical NOT
@end table

@subsubsection Bitwise (Lua 5.3+)

@table @code
@item &
AND
@item |
OR
@item ^^
XOR
@item ~
NOT (unary)
@item <<
Left shift
@item >>
Right shift
@end table

@subsubsection Other

@table @code
@item ..
String concatenation
@item #
Length operator
@item .
Table member access
@item [
Array indexing
@item :
Method call
@end table

@node Lua Grammar Overview
@section Grammar Overview

@subsection Program Structure

A Lua program consists of a sequence of statements:

@example
-- Global variable
local score = 0

-- Function definition
function add(a, b)
  return a + b
end

-- Main execution
score = add(10, 20)
print(score)
@end example

@subsection Blocks

Statements are grouped into blocks with @code{do...end}:

@example
do
  local x = 10
  print(x)
end
@end example

@subsection Chunks

A chunk is a sequence of statements, optionally ending with a return:

@example
if x > 0 then
  return x
end
return 0
@end example

@node Lua Statements
@section Statements

@subsection Local Variable Declaration

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
local name [= value]
local name1, name2 [= expr1, expr2]
@end example

@strong{Semantics}: Declares a variable with lexical scope. Optionally initializes to value(s).

@strong{Examples}:

@example
local x = 10
local y, z = 20, 30
local health = 100
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:move :target name :source value)}

@subsection Assignment

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
variable = expression
var1, var2 = expr1, expr2
@end example

@strong{Semantics}: Assigns expression value(s) to variable(s). Multiple assignment evaluates all RHS expressions before assignment.

@strong{Examples}:

@example
x = 100
player.x, player.y = 50, 75
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:move :target variable :source expression)}

@subsection Arithmetic

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
x = x + 1
x = x - y
total = total * factor
@end example

@strong{Semantics}: Shorthand available as @code{+=}, @code{-=}, @code{*=}, @code{/=}, @code{%=}, @code{^=}, @code{//=} (Lua 5.3+)

@strong{Examples}:

@example
x = x + 1
health = health - damage
mana = mana * 2
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:compute :op :+ :operands (x 1))}

@subsection Conditional

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
if condition then
  statements
elseif condition2 then
  statements
else
  statements
end
@end example

@strong{Semantics}: Executes matching branch. Only first true condition executes.

@strong{Examples}:

@example
if score > 1000 then
  print("Excellent!")
elseif score > 500 then
  print("Good!")
else
  print("Try again!")
end
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:if :condition cond :then then-block :else else-block)}

@subsection Loops

@strong{For Loop (Numeric)}:
@example
for i = 1, 10 [, step] do
  -- body
end
@end example

@strong{For Loop (Iterator)}:
@example
for k, v in pairs(table) do
  -- body
end
@end example

@strong{While Loop}:
@example
while condition do
  -- body
end
@end example

@strong{Repeat-Until Loop}:
@example
repeat
  -- body
until condition
@end example

@strong{Examples}:

@example
for i = 1, 10, 2 do
  print(i)
end

while health > 0 do
  process_turn()
end

repeat
  input = read()
until input ~= ""
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:perform :target loop-label)}

@subsection Function Definition

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
function name(param1, param2, ...)
  -- body
  return value
end
@end example

@strong{Semantics}: Defines a named function with parameters and optional return value(s).

@strong{Examples}:

@example
function factorial(n)
  if n <= 1 then
    return 1
  else
    return n * factorial(n - 1)
  end
end

function greet(name)
  print("Hello, " .. name)
end
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:invoke :method "name" :using params)}

@subsection Return Statement

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
return [value1, value2, ...]
@end example

@strong{Semantics}: Exits function, returning value(s) to caller. Default return is @code{nil}.

@strong{Examples}:

@example
function get-position()
  return x, y
end

function error-case()
  return nil
end
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:invoke :method "return" :using value)}

@subsection Table Operations

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
table[key] = value
value = table[key]
table.field = value
value = table.field
@end example

@strong{Semantics}: Accesses table (associative array / map) elements.

@strong{Examples}:

@example
player = {}
player["name"] = "Hero"
player.health = 100
print(player.name)
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:move :target (:aref table key) :source value)}

@node Lua Expressions
@section Expressions

@subsection Function Calls

@example
print("Hello")
add(5, 10)
player:take-damage(20)  -- Method call
@end example

@subsection Arithmetic Expressions

@example
x = 10 + 20 - 5
y = x * 2 / 3
z = 2 ^ 10
@end example

@subsection Comparison Expressions

@example
if x == 5 and y > 10 then
  -- ...
end

if not (x == 0) then
  -- ...
end
@end example

@subsection String Operations

@example
str = "Hello" .. " " .. "World"
len = #str
substr = string.sub(str, 1, 5)
@end example

@subsection Table Constructors

@example
t = {1, 2, 3}
dict = {x = 10, y = 20}
mixed = {1, name = "test", 2}
@end example

@subsection Lambda Functions

@example
square = function(x) return x * x end
add = function(a, b) return a + b end
@end example

@node Lua Type System
@section Type System

@subsection Primitive Types

@table @code
@item nil
Null/absence value
@item boolean
@code{true} or @code{false}
@item number
Integer or floating-point
@item string
Character sequence
@item function
Callable value
@end table

@subsection Composite Types

@table @code
@item table
Associative array / hashtable / object
@item thread
Coroutine (rarely used in EIGHTBOL context)
@item userdata
External C type (platform-specific)
@end table

@subsection Type Coercion

@itemize
@item Strings coerce to numbers in arithmetic contexts
@item Numbers coerce to strings in concatenation
@item @code{nil} and @code{false} are falsy; all else is truthy
@end itemize

@node Lua Examples
@section Examples

@subsection Complete Program Example

@example
function Particle(x, y)
  return {
    x = x, y = y,
    vx = 0, vy = 0,
    life = 30,
    update = function(self)
      self.x = self.x + self.vx
      self.y = self.y + self.vy
      self.vy = self.vy + 0.5  -- gravity
      self.life = self.life - 1
    end,
    is_alive = function(self)
      return self.life > 0
    end
  }
end

local particles = {}

function spawn_particle(x, y)
  table.insert(particles, Particle(x, y))
end

function update_particles()
  for i = #particles, 1, -1 do
    local p = particles[i]
    p:update()
    if not p:is_alive() then
      table.remove(particles, i)
    end
  end
end

-- Main loop
for frame = 1, 100 do
  if frame % 5 == 0 then
    spawn_particle(50, 50)
  end
  update_particles()
end
@end example

@subsection Canonical Use Cases

@strong{Game State Management}:
@example
local game = {
  score = 0,
  level = 1,
  add_score = function(self, points)
    self.score = self.score + points
  end
}
@end example

@strong{Event Handling}:
@example
function handle_input(key)
  if key == "up" then
    player.y = player.y - 1
  elseif key == "down" then
    player.y = player.y + 1
  end
end
@end example

@subsection Best Practices

@itemize
@item Use @code{local} for all variables unless global scope is intentional
@item Encapsulate related data and functions in tables
@item Use table methods with colon notation for OOP patterns
@item Avoid deep nesting; break complex functions into smaller ones
@item Use meaningful variable and function names
@item Document functions with comments explaining parameters and return values
@end itemize
